[LESSON]: Verification insights and user clarification patterns
- When a requirement calls for a property to be "suspended" (e.g., mutual exclusivity), verification must include not only that the suspension is possible but also that it is demonstrably exercised in at least one concrete edge case (to avoid vacuity).
- User prefers edge case scenarios to be formally encoded, with `expect 0` for negative cases (disallowed) and `expect 1` for positive cases (allowed/possible), and expects non-repetitive, precise requirements.
- Users may specify that certain model constraints (such as no role self-exclusion) are modeling choices, not system requirements, but still require that they be captured in assumptions for clarity.
- Immediate post-condition requirements (e.g., revocation of privilege or flag setting) must specify exact timing (e.g., "first Normal state after Emergency mode") for model clarity and testability.

