**Latest Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) environment supporting multiple users, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces authorization correctness, maintains delegation and permission consistency, and ensures safety and liveness under concurrency. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and objects have classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Requests are classified by the highest classification level of affected objects and must comply with user clearance. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance user requests. Delegation and revocation of roles are governed by clearance and administrative rules. The system includes an Emergency Module to manage exceptional scenarios, which alters standard behaviors under specific triggers and constraints. Emergency mode is modeled as a fixed duration but may terminate early if both administrators who activated it jointly deactivate it. The system guarantees that Emergency mode always eventually ends and the system returns to Normal mode.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Administrator membership is fixed; a user is an administrator if and only if they are in the Administrator set. isAdmin = True if and only if the user is an Administrator. Administrator status must be explicitly represented in the model.
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode and requires exactly two distinct administrators (emAdmins), both present in Administrator, acting concurrently to trigger entry. There is no requirement that two administrators must always exist in every session. The absence of sufficient administrators in a session must prevent Emergency mode entry, but does not violate any system invariant.
- Emergency mode is time-bounded (abstracted as a fixed duration), but may terminate early if both participating administrators jointly deactivate it; the system must always eventually return to Normal mode (liveness).
- FIFO ordering for request processing can be bypassed only during Emergency mode, and only for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended only during Emergency mode, and only if two distinct emAdmins are present; this suspension must be tested as a specific scenario. No role may be mutually exclusive with itself; role self-exclusion is not permitted in mutuallyExclusiveWith. This is a modeling choice, but must be enforced in the model and explicitly included as an assumption.
- Emergency privileges are granted only to the two distinct administrators who jointly triggered Emergency mode, only during Emergency mode, and must be revoked immediately at the transition to Normal mode for both administrators and in all subsequent Normal states.
- Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately at the Emergency-to-Normal transition. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode are not automatically revoked, but may still expire or be revoked by an administrator during Emergency mode.
- Only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger are required to update their credentials after Emergency mode ends; needsCredentialUpdate is set for them only in the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends, and must be unset (empty) in all other states.
- There is no constraint on the total number of administrators present in any session; the requirement for two emAdmins applies only to Emergency mode entry.
- In all verification runs and checks, the model must include at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles (i.e., there must exist two distinct roles r1 and r2 such that r1 is mutually exclusive with r2 and r1 ≠ r2) to ensure all mutual exclusion related requirements are tested non-trivially.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Trigger  
Exactly two distinct administrators, both present in the Administrator set and acting concurrently while the system is in Normal mode, are required to jointly trigger entry into Emergency mode. "Exactly two distinct" means the set of emAdmins must have cardinality two, both must be members of Administrator, and both must be distinct (no duplicates by signature). Emergency mode entry must be impossible in all other administrator configurations: with fewer than two administrators, with more than two administrators, with duplicate (identical) administrators, or with any non-administrator. All such cases—including attempted entry by only one administrator, by the same administrator counted twice, by more than two administrators, or by any non-admin—must be encoded and verified as explicit negative test scenarios using scenario predicates with `expect 0`, and shown to be disallowed by the model. The system must demonstrate that Emergency mode is possible only when exactly two distinct emAdmins act concurrently.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (bounded) time, abstracted as a fixed duration, but may terminate early if both participating emAdmins jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must always eventually terminate Emergency mode and return to Normal mode (liveness guarantee).

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1. During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize and process multiple high-classification requests simultaneously.  
R3.2. During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended only when exactly two distinct emAdmins are present. In all other configurations (including fewer or more than two administrators, duplicate administrators, or non-admins), mutual exclusivity must remain enforced. For all verification runs and checks, the model must include at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles (r1 ≠ r2, mutuallyExclusiveWith(r1, r2)), and there must exist an explicit scenario (edge case) where at least one user is assigned two mutually exclusive roles in Emergency mode with exactly two emAdmins present. All positive and negative edge cases for mutual exclusion suspension—including scenarios where a user holds two mutually exclusive roles, and cases where mutual exclusivity remains enforced—must be encoded and verified using explicit scenario predicates (`expect 1` for the positive case, `expect 0` for the negative cases). Mutual exclusion suspension is only permitted in Emergency mode with exactly two emAdmins present.
R3.3. During Emergency mode, only the two distinct administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger temporarily hold emergency privileges, and only during Emergency mode.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1. After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked immediately for both administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger, starting from the first Normal state and in all subsequent Normal states. EmergencyPrivilege for both emAdmins must not be present in any Normal state after Emergency mode ends.
R4.2. After Emergency mode ends, any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately. Delegated roles from before Emergency mode remain subject to normal expiration or administrator revocation, including during Emergency mode.  
R4.3. After Emergency mode ends, needsCredentialUpdate must be set to true for only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger, and only in the first Normal state after Emergency mode ends. needsCredentialUpdate must be empty (false for all users) in all other states, including during Emergency mode and all other Normal states.

NEWLY DISCOVERED REQUIREMENT GAPS
==================================
- All negative test scenarios for Emergency mode entry (fewer than two administrators, more than two administrators, duplicates, non-admins, or lack of concurrency) must be defined as explicit scenario predicates, each with `expect 0` to ensure no instance is possible. These must be run and verified in the model.
- The system must avoid vacuity in test scenarios: negative scenarios must be encoded as explicit scenario predicates with `expect 0`, and the positive scenario (exactly two distinct emAdmins) as an assertion or predicate with `expect 1`, to demonstrate model rejection or acceptance as required.
- Emergency mode entry and all related behaviors and suspensions (such as mutual exclusivity) are predicated on the presence of exactly two distinct emAdmins; these preconditions must be enforced and explicitly tested.
- For mutual exclusion suspension, all relevant edge cases (including a user holding two mutually exclusive roles in Emergency mode, and that no role is ever mutually exclusive with itself) must be encoded and verified using explicit scenario predicates and assertions, and their results must be explicitly checked in the model.
- For all verification runs and checks, the model must be constructed so that at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles (r1 ≠ r2, mutuallyExclusiveWith(r1, r2)) exists in scope, to ensure all mutual exclusion scenarios are tested non-trivially and vacuity is avoided.

PROVISIONAL ASSUMPTIONS FOR VERIFICATION
========================================
- For purposes of verification, scenarios with fewer than two, exactly two, more than two, duplicate, and non-admin administrators attempting to trigger Emergency mode are explicitly modeled as separate edge cases. Only the case with exactly two distinct emAdmins acting concurrently may result in Emergency mode being entered; all others must not.
- For concurrency modeling, "acting concurrently" is abstracted as a single transition step for both administrators.
- Constraints on the number and identity of administrators participating in Emergency mode entry are enforced through explicit scenario predicates and negative test cases, rather than by restricting the administrator count in command signatures. This avoids syntax errors and improves clarity.
- For mutual exclusivity modeling, no role is permitted to be mutually exclusive with itself (role self-exclusion is disallowed). This must be enforced in the model as a modeling assumption.
- Verification of mutual exclusion suspension must explicitly include both positive and negative edge scenarios as required above, and for all runs/checks the scope must include at least one mutually exclusive role pair.
